# <%= projectName %>

HTTP-first React SSR starter generated by `fluo new --starter react-vite-ssr`.

Requires Node.js `>=24.0.0 <27`. Use Node 24 LTS for local development, CI, and container images. The server build targets `node24` and uses `@types/node@^24.0.0`.

## First two pages

```sh
<%= installCommand %>
<%= devCommand %>
```

The generated `dev` script runs `fluo dev`, as does the installed `fluo` binary invoked
directly. After installation, neither path requires a manual production build: the CLI
loads the server entry through project-local Vite SSR transforms and serves client
modules and CSS through Vite middleware on the Fastify development server behind a
persistent same-origin gateway. Source
edits use the same-origin Vite WebSocket and React Fast Refresh for supported component
boundaries, while CSS updates without replacing the document. Vite-owned browser modules
do not restart the app child; each directly requested page loads its current SSR component
through Vite after HTTP route and DTO validation. Server-only edits drain and
replace the app behind the gateway; watched `.env` and Vite config edits replace
the dev process. No production `dist` or client build manifest is needed for `dev`; `build` and
`start` remain the separate production path.

Keep component-only exports in refreshable modules. Changing hook order or incompatible
exports can require a React remount or a full document reload; state is preserved only when
React recognizes the component boundary. A syntax error appears in the Vite browser overlay
and terminal; correct the file in the same dev session to resume updates. Existing apps
generated without `@vitejs/plugin-react` or `src/entry-client-dev.ts` should follow the
[React dev HMR migration](https://github.com/fluojs/fluo/blob/main/docs/getting-started/migrate-react-dev-hmr.md) rather than adding another Vite process.

Open `http://127.0.0.1:3000/products/sku-42?preview=true`, then follow
the Search catalog link to `/search?q=catalog`. Edit `src/page.tsx` and
`src/page-search.tsx`; their UI and hydrated interaction share the same server/client
document, provider, and page slot. The shell stays mounted during approved soft navigation;
page-local state resets when a new destination mounts. Network, HTTP 5xx, and recoverable mapped
page import failures or incompatible build identities preserve the approved page and shell with `Retry navigation` and
`Open full document` controls outside the page slot. Before hydration, with JavaScript disabled,
on modified clicks, and on unavailable destinations, links remain native anchors.

The decorated route remains explicit in `src/app.ts`:

```ts
@Router('/products')
class ProductPageRouter {
  @Path('/:sku')
  @RequestDto(ProductPageRequest)
  async show(input: ProductPageRequest) {
    const props = { sku: input.sku, preview: input.preview === 'true' };
    const page = options.loadPage
      ? await options.loadPage('./page.tsx', props)
      : createElement(ProductPage, props);
    return ReactNavigationPage.create(page, {
      module: './page.tsx',
      props,
    });
  }
}
```

This handler still runs through `@fluojs/http` matching, DTO binding, validation, middleware,
guards, interceptors, request scopes, and not-found behavior before the application renderer sees
the returned page. The search handler likewise validates its `q` query DTO and selects
`./page-search.tsx` with one `query` prop. In development, `options.loadPage` loads
either component through Vite for fresh SSR after HTTP validation. To add an ordinary third page, create
`src/page-about.tsx` with a default component accepting `Record<string, unknown>`,
then add its explicit `@Router`/`@Path` handler and DTO in `src/app.ts`; select
`ReactNavigationPage.create(createElement(AboutPage, props), {
module: './page-about.tsx', props })` and register that router in `ReactModule.forRoot`.
No renderer, client entry, manifest plumbing, or client matcher edits are needed: both entries
pick up `./page*.tsx` through Vite's build-produced importer map. The server checks that
the selected module is present before committing the document; the browser only imports
approved keys. Only JSON data belongs in `props`; never pass DI instances, server-only
modules, or secrets. Decorator-bearing application declarations stay in `.ts` files so the
Vite transform handles them; JSX rendering stays in `.tsx` modules such as `src/page.tsx`.

