<%= testBrowserCommand %>
```

`<%= testBrowserCommand %>` starts the built Fastify server and verifies the first HTTP response, emitted
assets, hydration, two-page soft navigation, network/5xx failure and fresh retry plus mapped
page import failure with a live shell resource, and native fallback without console warnings
or errors. `tests/deployment-transition.spec.ts` independently builds A and B outputs,
switches a same-origin HTTP surface, verifies retained/missing hashed assets and requires
an explicit document choice before replacing an incompatible A shell.
Install the Playwright Chrome browser first when your environment does not already provide it.

## Application-owned wiring

Read these files only when you need to change the default composition:

- `src/app.ts` owns explicit HTTP routes and registers the application renderer.
- `src/entry-server.tsx` is the application-owned `ReactPageRenderer`. It turns the already-loaded
  Vite manifest into hydration assets and returns `ReactServerEntry` through
  `createReactServerEntry(...)`. Replace this renderer when the application needs another document
  or explicit asset policy.
- `src/react-app.tsx` is the shared server/client document. It owns
  `ReactClientRouterProvider`, the request route snapshot, a persistent shell and page slot.
- `src/entry-client.tsx` loads the handler-selected built module from the same static
  importer map, then hydrates that document. It reports a mismatch with the exact entry and
  shared-composition files to compare. The HTTP-owned initial payload has a 64 KiB escaped
  JSON limit and is validated before HTML response commit.
- `src/main.ts` loads `dist/client/.vite/manifest.json` through `src/load-manifest.ts` for
  production `start`. In `fluo dev`, the CLI loads the same app module through Vite SSR
  and proxies its ephemeral Fastify backend through the persistent gateway; Vite's
  development asset middleware runs on that backend. No build manifest is read.
  Missing or malformed production build output points back to `<%= buildCommand %>` and
  `vite.client.config.ts`.

`@fluojs/react/vite` only parses the manifest value loaded by the application. It does not discover
files, run Vite, serve assets, or move route ownership out of `@fluojs/http`. The Fastify asset
controller is a minimal local starter boundary; production deployments should serve `dist/client`
through their normal static-file or CDN infrastructure.
Its `/assets/` bootstrap and lazy chunks are content-hashed, with immutable asset cache
headers and private non-reusable HTML/navigation. Publish all B assets before switching B
server/manifest, retain A for your specified old-tab window and monitor
`X-Fluo-Asset-Status: missing` on 404. For v1-to-v2 protocol migration and the
application/host responsibility boundary, see
https://github.com/fluojs/fluo/blob/main/docs/getting-started/migrate-react-production-assets.md
and https://github.com/fluojs/fluo/blob/main/docs/guides/react-production-deployment.md.
The client manifest retains the server entry identity for `@fluojs/react/vite`, but its client
output is an empty marker; the actual `src/entry-server.tsx` builds only into `dist/server`.
Only page components from `src/page*.tsx` enter browser chunks. Do not import server services
from those shared page modules.
The shell opts into `ReactNavigationExperience` from `@fluojs/react/client`. It retains the
last approved page during pending navigation, announces status, resets approved render errors
inside the page slot, and applies focus/scroll defaults. `@PageMetadata(...)` on each HTTP
handler supplies bounded page-owned title, description, and canonical-link entries to the
initial document and later destinations. To replace the effect, set `onApprovedNavigation`
on that composition. A page render reset is local; #3864's transport retry instead obtains
fresh HTTP approval. React 19 and React DOM 19 are required for page-owned head hoisting.
Direct/JavaScript-disabled errors still use HTTP status and representation.

`Link` remains a real anchor; `router.push(...)` and compatible links request HTTP-approved JSON
before updating the URL and page slot. This starter explicitly selects `failurePolicy` for
network/5xx and recoverable mapped import failures, preserving the approved shell and offering
fresh `router.retry()` and explicit `router.openDocument()`. Auth refusal, redirects, missing
pages, DTO and invalid payloads, and unsupported module keys retain document fallback;
low-level providers without a policy retain that fallback for every non-cancelled failure.
Explicit reload, logout, and tab termination do not preserve
a resource. This starter intentionally excludes RSC, Server Functions, file routing, a client
route table, SPA document swapping, prefetch, and a data cache.
